	'pe_p' => 'Seven people run the reviews, and they look at a desk the same way: watch first, change the least, write it down. Ilse Varga leads. The others cover workstation fit, light and layout, movement, accessibility, the visit itself and the written plan.',
	'pe_t' => 'Seven people, one way of looking',
	'privacy_0_h' => 'What we collect, and why',
	'privacy_0_p1' => 'Through the enquiry form we collect the name, email address, company, number of desks, reason for writing, preferred weeks and message that you send, together with the consent line that Katachi may contact you about that request. We use those details to reply, to offer a scoping call, and to book a visit if you ask for one. Through the Next openings list we collect an email address only, and we use it to send the monthly note: open visit days and one desk tip, on the first Monday of each month. We do not buy address lists, and we do not pass either set of details to a furniture seller. The studio address is Unit 4, 18 Foundry Row, Northvale. The email for either kind of request is hello@katachi-ergonomics.example. The phone is 010 736 2240.',
	'privacy_1_h' => 'How long we keep it',
	'privacy_1_p1' => 'Enquiry details are kept for as long as the conversation and the visit, if there is one, need them, and then for the invoicing period that follows the plan. Invoices go out after the plan is delivered, and payment is due in thirty days, so the correspondence stays at least through that. If no visit is booked, we keep the enquiry only as long as a reply might still be owed, and then we delete it. Next openings addresses are kept until you use the leave link in an email. We do not keep a copy of the list for marketing after you have left. Visit notes and written plans are working papers for your team. They are not published, and company names in the public field notes are withheld.',
	'privacy_2_h' => 'Your rights',
	'privacy_2_p1' => 'You can ask what we hold about you, ask us to correct it, or ask us to delete it. For the Next openings list, the leave link in every email is the direct way off. For an enquiry, write to hello@katachi-ergonomics.example and say what you want done. We reply within two working days, the same window we use for a new booking request. Leaving the list does not cancel a review. Cancelling a review follows the terms: free up to five working days before the visit day, and 25 percent of the fee after that. We do not use the form or the list to send furniture offers. The only regular mail from the list is the monthly note.',
	'privacy_l' => 'PRIVACY',
	'privacy_ld' => 'This page says what we collect through the enquiry form and the Next openings list, why we keep it, how long it stays, and what you can ask us to do with it.',
	'privacy_t' => 'Privacy',
	'review_0_h' => 'What readiness means',
	'review_0_p1' => 'Readiness means the floor is understood before money is spent on moving it, furnishing it, or bringing people back to it. Office and operations managers, and HR and facilities leads, ask us in when a team of five to sixty people is about to change the room or the week. We do not start from a preferred product. We start from the person at the desk. The review has three parts: workstation fit, light, noise and layout, and movement through the day. You receive a written plan, one page per desk and a floor summary, within five working days. A purchase list is included only if what you already have cannot be adjusted. Six weeks later we return for a ninety-minute check.',
	'review_10_h' => 'Standing and sitting',
	'review_10_p1' => 'We look at how long a person stays in one position, and at whether the desk they have can change height. Standing is not treated as the better half of the day. A floor that bought sit-stand desks and then stood for whole shifts is a floor we have seen; the useful change was alternation that matched the work, not more time on foot. What we usually change is the way this person uses the desk they already own: a pattern of sitting and standing written on their page, and the chair or perch they use when they come down. Anti-fatigue mats and other kit are named only where the day on foot makes them relevant. We do not claim a health result from the pattern. We record what was agreed.',
	'review_11_h' => 'Breaks and movement',
	'review_11_p1' => 'Breaks and movement are about the day around the desk. We look at whether the person can leave the chair, where they go, and whether the work, a queue, a call, a drawing, actually lets them. Routes between desks, the quiet room, and the way out of the floor are part of the note. What we usually change is a written cue the team can keep: when a break sits in this job, and which existing route makes it a real break rather than a lap of the same aisle. We do not issue a wellness programme, and we do not time people\'s lunches. If pain or a medical condition is the reason someone cannot move as the work asks, we say so plainly and refer to a physiotherapist or a doctor.',
	'review_12_h' => 'What you receive',
	'review_12_p1' => 'Every readiness review includes the scoping call, the visit day, the written plan and the six-week check. The plan arrives within five working days. It holds one page per desk and a floor summary. Each desk page records what we looked at and what we changed or recommend changing. The floor summary records light, noise, routes and anything that does not belong to a single chair. A purchase list is added only if it is needed. The six-week check is ninety minutes on site, to see what has stayed in place. The findings conversation with the manager happens at 16:30 on the visit day, so the plan is not the first time you hear what we saw. Single-desk and remote notes are shorter, and they follow the fee you booked.',
	'review_13_h' => 'What we will not do',
	'review_13_p1' => 'We will not sell you furniture. We will not take a commission on furniture you buy from anyone else. We will not diagnose a medical condition. The review is advice about the desk, the room and the working day. It is not treatment. Where a person needs a diagnosis, we refer to a physiotherapist or a doctor, including when the lead consultant\'s own background is physiotherapy. We will not promise that shoulders, feet or concentration will change by a given date. Field notes on this site report what people told us at a six-week check. They are not a forecast for your floor. If you want a readiness review, the scoping call is free, thirty minutes by phone or video, and the visit is booked from there.',
	'review_1_h' => 'Workstation fit',
	'review_1_n' => '01 / 03',
	'review_1_p1' => 'Workstation fit is the first part of the review, and it stays at the desk itself. We look at the chair and the seat, the desk height, the screen and the keyboard, and the way a laptop is set when the laptop is the computer. The question is whether this person, at this desk, can work without the furniture fighting them. Adjustments that the chair and the monitor arm already allow are made in the twenty-minute session when they are safe and obvious. Anything that needs a purchase is named on that person\'s page, not urged in the room. The four checks under this part are chair and seat, desk height, screen and keyboard, and laptop set-ups.',
	'review_2_h' => 'Light, noise and layout',
	'review_2_n' => '02 / 03',
	'review_2_p1' => 'The second part lifts the view from one chair to the room. On the visit morning, from 08:30 to 10:00, we walk the floor for light, noise, routes and how the room is used. A bright window can make a good screen unreadable. A quiet desk can sit on a route that never stays quiet. Layout is not a drawing in this part; it is where people actually stand, pass and gather while the work is going on. What we usually change is position: the screen relative to the window, the person relative to the aisle, the shared kit relative to the people who share it. Buying a new lamp or a new screen is a later line, and only if the move on the floor plan is not enough.',
	'review_3_h' => 'Movement through the day',
	'review_3_n' => '03 / 03',
	'review_3_p1' => 'The third part is movement through the day: standing and sitting, breaks, and the routes that already exist between desks, rooms and the way out. A sit-stand desk that stays at standing height for a whole shift is not, by itself, a varied day. A chair that fits does not help a person who never leaves it between the start of work and the findings we write down. We watch what the work allows. Some jobs pin someone to a headset and a queue. The plan then says what can change inside that constraint, and what cannot. We do not prescribe a medical programme, and we do not promise that a new rhythm will feel a certain way. We write the rhythm the person can actually keep.',
	'review_4_h' => 'Chair and seat',
	'review_4_p1' => 'At the chair we look at the person who is sitting in it today, not at the chair\'s reputation. Seat height, seat depth, the position of the back, and the armrests relative to the elbows are the usual points. We ask the person to work as they normally do for a few minutes, and we watch where the shoulders and the feet settle. What we usually change is the setting of the chair that is already there: a lever, a tension, an armrest brought down or in. If the chair has been set for someone else, resetting it is the work. A different chair is named only when this one cannot be made to fit. That judgement goes in the written note. We do not sell the replacement, and we do not take a commission if you buy one.',
	'review_5_h' => 'Desk height',
	'review_5_p1' => 'Desk height is read from the person, not from a standard sticker. We look at the elbow once the shoulders are dropped, and at whether the feet are supported, on the floor or on a footrest the team already has. A sit-stand desk is measured at the height the person is actually using, which is sometimes not the height they think they use. What we usually change is the surface height, and the chair height that has to move with it. If the desk cannot reach a workable height for that person, the page says so, and the purchase list, if there is one, says what range is missing. Small fixes are made on the spot during the twenty minutes. The rest waits for the plan.',
	'review_6_h' => 'Screen and keyboard',
	'review_6_p1' => 'For the screen we look at the top edge against the eye line, the distance the person has chosen, and whether glare from a window is washing the panel. The keyboard is part of the same look: how far it sits from the body, and whether the shoulders lift to reach it. The mark we use in the room is simple. A hand held flat at the top of the monitor tells us, and the person, where the screen is relative to the eyes. What we usually change is the monitor arm or the stand already on the desk, and the place of the keyboard. A new screen is not the first recommendation. If the arm cannot rise far enough, that limit is written down. We are watching the set-up the person will use tomorrow morning.',
	'review_7_h' => 'Laptop set-ups',
	'review_7_p1' => 'A laptop used as the only screen often puts the eyes on a low panel and the hands on a keyboard that cannot move. We look at whether the screen can reach eye height, and whether a separate keyboard and pointing device are already in the drawer. What we usually change is the arrangement: raise the laptop, bring in a separate keyboard if one is on site, and write the rule on that person\'s page. The rule we use is the one we can check later, the laptop screen at eye height, not a brand of stand. Remote desk reviews, forty minutes by video, use the same look when the desk is at home. A purchase list appears only if the home or the office has nothing that will raise the screen.',
	'review_8_h' => 'Light and glare',
	'review_8_p1' => 'Light is watched at the desk, not averaged across the floor. We look at the window beside or behind the screen, at the brightness on the desk surface, and at whether the person is squinting, tilting or moving their head to escape a reflection. A small meter reading can help the conversation; the decision still comes from where the person is sitting. What we usually change is orientation. Turn the screen, shift the desk, or move the person to a place on the same floor where the window is no longer in the panel. We do not start by specifying blinds, films or new lamps. If none of the existing places work for that role, the floor summary says what the room cannot do.',
	'review_9_h' => 'Noise and layout',
	'review_9_p1' => 'Noise and layout are one check because they describe the same floor. We listen for the voices, headsets and routes that pass a desk, and we watch where work actually gathers: a printer, a question, a shared table. The morning walk, from 08:30 to 10:00, is when this is clearest, while people are in. What we usually change is a place or a small piece of kit the team already relies on, written as a floor note rather than a personal fault. A desk on a route may need a different neighbour, not a different chair. We do not redesign the tenancy. We describe how this room is used, and the few moves that would make the described work easier to hear and to reach.',
	'review_checks_h' => 'Eight checks, one desk at a time',
	'review_ck1_n' => 'CHECK 01',
