	'tt_c1' => 'Time',
	'tt_c2' => 'What happens',
	'v02_a' => 'Walk the floor — details',
	'v02_l' => 'STEP 02',
	'v02_p' => 'Between 08:30 and 10:00 we walk the floor: light, noise, routes and how the room is used. People keep working. The walk gives us the shape of the place before the desk sessions begin.',
	'v02_t' => 'Walk the floor',
	'v03_a' => 'Sit with each desk — details',
	'v03_l' => 'STEP 03',
	'v03_p' => 'Each person gives us twenty minutes at their own desk. We look at the chair, the screen and the way they sit, and we make small fixes on the spot. One consultant covers up to fifteen desks in the day.',
	'v03_t' => 'Sit with each desk',
	'v04_a' => 'Write it down, then check — details',
	'v04_l' => 'STEP 04',
	'v04_p' => 'At 16:30 we sit with the manager and talk through what we found. The written plan arrives within 5 working days, one page for each desk plus a floor summary. Six weeks later we return for a 90-minute check on site.',
	'v04_t' => 'Write it down, then check',
	'visit_0_h' => 'Scoping call',
	'visit_0_n' => 'STEP 01',
	'visit_0_p1' => 'The scoping call is thirty minutes, by phone or video, and it is free. We agree which desks are in the review, the date of the visit, and what readiness means for this floor. An office move, a furniture purchase and a return to the office are the usual reasons; if the reason is something else, say so and we will tell you whether a readiness review is the right piece of work. You will know the fee before the call ends: a small team up to ten desks, a floor of eleven to thirty, a single desk, or a remote review. Visit days are Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday. They fill about three weeks ahead. We reply to a written enquiry within two working days and offer this call. Nothing is invoiced for the conversation itself.',
	'visit_1_h' => 'Walk the floor',
	'visit_1_n' => 'STEP 02',
	'visit_1_p1' => 'The visit day starts at 08:30. From 08:30 to 10:00 we walk the floor: light, noise, routes, and how the room is used while people are working. This is not a tour of empty furniture. We want the ordinary morning, including the places where conversation gathers and the desks that sit in glare. A floor plan, or a phone photo of one, is what we carry so the walk can be tied to rooms you recognise. The walk is step 02 because the desk sessions make more sense once the room is clear. Notes from the aisle go into the floor summary, not only into the individual pages. If two consultants are on a larger floor, both walk before either sits down to a chair. The day runs until 17:00.',
	'visit_2_h' => 'Sit with each desk',
	'visit_2_n' => 'STEP 03',
	'visit_2_p1' => 'After the walk, we sit with each desk. Twenty minutes per person, at their own desk, while colleagues keep working. One consultant sees up to fifteen desks in the day. A floor of eleven to thirty desks is staffed with two consultants so the sessions still fit. We look at the chair, the desk height, the screen, the keyboard and the laptop if that is the machine in use. Small fixes are made on the spot: a lever, an arm, a keyboard brought closer. Anything that should not be improvised is written down and left for the plan. Each person needs only those twenty minutes. The rest of their day is their own. We need a list of desks and names beforehand so nobody is guessed at from a vacant chair.',
	'visit_3_h' => 'Write it down, then check',
	'visit_3_n' => 'STEP 04',
	'visit_3_p1' => 'At 16:30 on the visit day we hold the findings conversation with the manager. The written plan follows within five working days: one page per desk and a floor summary. The report itself is written over the next two days; Friday at our studio is kept for that writing. Six weeks later we return for the check, ninety minutes on site, to see what is still in place and to hear what people at the desks say about the change. The check is part of the readiness review fee, not an extra visit to be negotiated later. A purchase list is in the plan only if adjustment was not enough. We do not stay to sell what is on that list. Adjustment sessions, if you want hands on site for a half day after the plan, are booked separately.',
	'visit_4_h' => 'Day timetable',
	'visit_4_p1' => 'Visit days are Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, 08:30 to 17:00 at your site. At 08:30 we arrive and start the floor walk. The walk runs until 10:00 and covers light, noise, routes and how the room is used. From the end of the walk until the late afternoon, each person has a twenty-minute session at their own desk. A consultant takes up to fifteen of those sessions in a day. At 16:30 we sit with the manager for the findings conversation. The day ends at 17:00. Friday is reports only; we are not on a client floor. Saturday, Sunday and public holidays are closed. The written plan is delivered within five working days of the visit, with the drafting done over the two days that follow the day on site.',
	'visit_5_h' => 'What we need from you',
	'visit_5_p1' => 'Four things make the day work, and we ask for them before we travel. A floor plan, or a phone photo of the floor plan, so the morning walk can be named against rooms. A list of desks and names, so each twenty-minute session is with the person who uses that desk. One quiet room for twenty minutes, used for the 16:30 findings conversation and for anything that should not be said in the aisle. A contact on the day, someone who can open the floor and point us to the list if a name has changed that morning. Tom Brennan, the visit coordinator, collects these before the date. Questionnaires, when we use them, come from him as well. We do not need the office cleared.',
	'visit_6_h' => 'What your team does',
	'visit_6_p1' => 'Your team keeps working. We are guests on a live floor, not the reason for a shutdown. Each person gives us twenty minutes at their own desk and then returns to the job. Managers do not need to attend the desk sessions. They do need to be there at 16:30 for the findings conversation, and they need to have named a contact who is present for the whole visit day. People should use their usual chair, their usual screen and their usual shoes. A desk tidied into a showroom tells us less than a desk mid-task. If someone is out, say so on the list; we would rather skip a desk than review a chair with nobody in it. Hybrid teams can mix a visit day for the office desks with remote reviews for the people at home.',
	'visit_7_h' => 'After the visit',
	'visit_7_p1' => 'The plan arrives within five working days. Read the page that belongs to each desk with the person who sits there, and read the floor summary once, as a room. If a purchase list is attached, it is a list of gaps, not an order form. We do not sell the items and we do not take a commission. An adjustment session can be booked after the plan if you want a half day on site fitting chairs, screens and keyboards; that session is a separate fee. Six weeks after the visit we come back for ninety minutes. The check is the moment we hear what has held and what has drifted. Travel within 40 km of Northvale is included in the review. Beyond that, travel is charged at €0.45 per kilometre.',
	'visit_h' => 'How the visit is run',
	'visit_l' => 'THE VISIT',
	'visit_ld' => 'One call, one day on your floor, one written plan. Step 01 is a free thirty-minute scoping call; the rest happens at your desks.',
	'visit_line' => 'Step 01 is a free 30-minute scoping call; the rest happens at your desks.',
	'visit_need1' => 'A floor plan, or a phone photo of one',
	'visit_need2' => 'A list of desks and names',
	'visit_need3' => 'One quiet room for 20 minutes',
	'visit_need4' => 'A contact on the day',
	'visit_st' => 'One call, one day on your floor, one written plan.',
	'visit_t' => 'The Visit',
	'waitlist_0_h' => 'What you get',
	'waitlist_0_n' => '01 / 03',
	'waitlist_0_p1' => 'The Next openings list is a short note, not a newsletter of essays. Each one carries the month\'s open visit days and one desk tip. The days are the Tuesdays, Wednesdays and Thursdays we still have, said plainly so you can see whether a review can land before a move, a purchase or a return to the office. The desk tip is one observation from recent visits, the sort of thing we would say in the first ten minutes at a desk: a screen, a chair set for someone else, a day spent standing. It is not a product. Joining the list does not book a review. Booking still starts with a note to us and a free scoping call.',
	'waitlist_1_h' => 'When it arrives',
	'waitlist_1_n' => '02 / 03',
	'waitlist_1_p1' => 'The note goes out on the first Monday of each month. It is about three minutes\' read. We started the list in 2023 so that teams who could not find a date in the current window would hear when the next month opened, without having to write and ask. If a week fills after the note has gone, the page you book from is still the right place to check; the list is a first hearing, not a live board. There is no mid-month edition. One Monday, one note, the open days and a single tip. If you need a date sooner than the next first Monday, write anyway. We reply within two working days.',
	'waitlist_2_h' => 'How to leave',
	'waitlist_2_n' => '03 / 03',
	'waitlist_2_p1' => 'Every email has one link that takes you off the list. Use it and you stop receiving the note. You do not need to write to us to leave, and leaving does not affect a review you have already booked. We use the address only to send this note. The form asks for an email address and nothing else. If the form opens your email app instead, send the message to join the list; you are not on it yet. The subject line for that message is Join the Next openings list. The address is hello@katachi-ergonomics.example.',
	'waitlist_l' => 'NEXT OPENINGS',
	'waitlist_ld' => 'Visit days fill about three weeks ahead. Join the list and hear first when a month opens.',
	'waitlist_t' => 'Next openings',
	'wl_l' => 'JOIN THE LIST',
	'wl_t' => 'Hear first when a month opens',
	'wm_a' => 'KATACHI',
